(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-viral-and-fungal-infections-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cutaneous Viral and Fungal Infections Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does herpetic whitlow mimic?","choices":["Bacterial felon or paronychia, contact dermatitis, and blistering dactylitis","Squamous cell carcinoma, seborrheic keratosis, and molluscum contagiosum","Basal cell carcinoma, sebaceous hyperplasia, and condyloma acuminatum","Chancroid, syphilis, trauma, and candidiasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — those are the three named mimics. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which variations of verruca does the deck list?","choices":["Verruca vulgaris, verruca plantaris, and condyloma acuminatum only","Verruca vulgaris, verruca plana, verruca plantaris, mosaic warts, and genital warts","Verruca vulgaris, verruca plana, verruca plantaris, and molluscum contagiosum","Verruca vulgaris, seborrheic keratosis, and squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — five variants. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"Which condition does the deck flag in a clinical pearl as often confused with tinea corporis?","choices":["Fixed drug eruption","Nummular eczema","Psoriasis","Discoid lupus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck gives it its own pearl slide. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"At what ages does verruca vulgaris occur most often, and what is its natural history?","choices":["Most often between 40 and 60 years, with spontaneous resolution","Most often between 5 and 20 years, with progression to carcinoma","Most often after the age of 65, with lifelong persistence once acquired","Most often between 5 and 20 years, with spontaneous resolution"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — common warts usually resolve on their own. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"Where do the lesions of pityriasis versicolor favour, and what do they look like?","choices":["Palms and soles; thick silvery scaling plaques","Body folds only; well demarcated patches with satellite pustules","Sun-exposed face and hands; rough sandpaper texture","Trunk, neck and proximal arms; finely scaling macules and patches"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — hypopigmented, hyperpigmented or pink, velvety, from 4 to 5 millimetres up to large confluent areas. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Why must fungus be confirmed before starting oral therapy for a dystrophic nail?","choices":["Many dystrophic nails are not fungal","Oral antifungals interfere with the nail sampling assay","Confirmation is needed for insurance approval","The organism cannot be cultured once treatment has started for six weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck gives a whole slide to dystrophic nails that are not onychomycosis. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How does tinea manuum present on the dorsum of the hand compared with the palm?","choices":["The dorsum shows an annular plaque like tinea corporis; the palm is hyperkeratotic like tinea pedis","The dorsum is hyperkeratotic like tinea pedis; the palm shows an annular plaque like tinea corporis","Both surfaces show vesicles on an erythematous base","Both surfaces show maceration and fissuring in the web spaces"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the palm becomes thickened, dry and scaly, and both palms and soles can be infected at once. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the first step in managing tinea incognito, and what should you warn about?","choices":["Biopsy before any change in treatment","Stop the corticosteroid or calcineurin inhibitor; expect a rebound","Continue the corticosteroid while adding an antifungal, to prevent rebound","Start oral therapy immediately in every case"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — inflammation may rebound after withdrawal. Then obtain potassium hydroxide or culture from an active edge. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What causes pityriasis versicolor, and is it contagious?","choices":["An autoimmune destruction of melanocytes; it is not contagious","Overgrowth of Malassezia, which normally lives on skin; NOT contagious","Infection with Candida albicans acquired from close contact; it is contagious","Infection with Trichophyton rubrum from shared towels; it is contagious"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — lipid-dependent Malassezia species. More common with heat, humidity, oily skin, sweating, immunosuppression or corticosteroid exposure. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What does the deck say about school exclusion in tinea capitis?","choices":["The child must be excluded until fungal culture is negative","The child must be excluded for the full duration of oral therapy","Exclusion is required only if a kerion is present","Generally unnecessary once effective therapy has begun"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — follow local policy. And complete the oral course even when itching or scaling improves early. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the limitation of viral culture for herpes simplex?","choices":["It is highly sensitive but very slow, so a negative result reliably excludes infection once it returns","It cannot distinguish type 1 from type 2, although its sensitivity is equal to amplification testing","It is only valid in the first episode, and it performs better than amplification in recurrent lesions","It is less sensitive, especially in healing or recurrent lesions, and a negative result does not exclude infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a negative culture cannot rule out herpes. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"What characterises the pre-eruptive phase of herpes zoster, and how long before the lesions?","choices":["Fever and regional lymphadenopathy without any dermatomal pain, with lesions appearing about two weeks later","Dysesthesia or pain within the affected dermatome, with lesions appearing by 48 to 72 hours","A generalised pruritic eruption with lesions in multiple stages at once, with pain appearing only after 48 to 72 hours","Painless vesicles within the affected dermatome appearing first, with pain following about a week afterwards"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — malaise, myalgia, headache and photophobia may occur, and rarely fever. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What are the three criteria for establishing an id reaction?","choices":["Biopsy showing hyphae, positive culture, and resolution with topical steroid","Symmetrical distribution, sparing of the palms, and a history of atopy","Infection elsewhere, NO fungus at the id site, resolution when treated","Positive potassium hydroxide at both sites, a raised immunoglobulin E, and response to antihistamines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a dermatophyte infection on another part of the body, absence of fungal elements from the id reaction site, and resolution of the id reaction when the primary infection is treated. So the potassium hydroxide is positive at the primary site and negative at the distant one. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which groups have increased risk of complications from varicella?","choices":["Patients with diabetes, peripheral vascular disease, and nail trauma","Patients with atopic dermatitis, asthma and allergic rhinitis","Adults, pregnancy, newborn age, and immunocompromise","Preadolescent children and adolescents, and no one else"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those four groups are the ones the deck names. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What is Hutchinson sign, and what does its absence mean?","choices":["Lesions on the eyelid margin, which increase ocular risk; its absence does reliably exclude eye involvement entirely","A vesicular eruption of the external auditory canal, which increases ocular risk; its absence excludes eye involvement","Pain persisting beyond 90 days after the rash, which increases ocular risk; its absence excludes eye involvement","Lesions on the tip or side of the nose, which increase ocular risk; its absence does NOT exclude eye involvement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — herpes zoster ophthalmicus involves the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Describe the individual molluscum lesion and its typical size.","choices":["Discrete, rough-surfaced, soft, grayish, flat-topped papules averaging 10 to 15 millimetres","Confluent, scaly, erythematous plaques with an advancing border averaging 3 to 5 centimetres","Grouped, fluid-filled vesicles on an erythematous base averaging 1 to 2 millimetres","Discrete, smooth-surfaced, firm, flesh-coloured, dome-shaped pearly papules averaging 3 to 5 millimetres"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — with characteristic central umbilication. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 134"},{"q":"Who gets tinea cruris, what is it commonly called, and what does it often coexist with?","choices":["More common in women; jock itch; often coexists with tinea capitis","Equally common in both sexes; ringworm; often coexists with onychomycosis","More common in children; athlete's foot; often coexists with tinea barbae","More common in men; jock itch; often coexists with tinea pedis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why the deck says to treat the coexisting foot infection. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for intertrigo and cutaneous candidiasis?","choices":["Obesity, diabetes, incontinence, occlusion, immobility, antibiotics","Age over fifty, sun exposure, fair skin, red tattoo ink and previous cryotherapy","Contact with pets, shared hats and combs, and asymptomatic carriers","Heat, humidity, oily skin, sweating and corticosteroid exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and immunosuppression. Common sites are inframammary, axillary, abdominal, inguinal, perineal and interdigital folds. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is the standard dosing interval for recombinant zoster vaccine, and when may it be shortened?","choices":["One month for everyone; for immunocompetent patients the second dose may be delayed to six months when that is more convenient","Two to six months; the interval may be shortened only for adults aged 80 and over, and never for immunocompromised patients","Two to six months; for immunocompromised patients the second dose may be given one to two months after the first when faster completion is beneficial","Six to twelve months; for immunocompromised patients the second dose may never be given early, whatever the clinical urgency or degree of immunosuppression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the shortened interval is specific to that group. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"What produces the annular outline that gives tinea corporis the name ringworm?","choices":["Central ulceration with a rolled edge","Peripheral hyperpigmentation around a pale centre","Progressive central clearing","A raised border of confluent pustules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lesion is a circular, sharply circumscribed, slightly erythematous, dry scaly patch or plaque. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Besides alopecia and scaly patches, which examination finding is often present in tinea capitis?","choices":["Nail pitting","Coral-red fluorescence under a Wood lamp","Lymphadenopathy","Fever above 38 degrees Celsius"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — alongside red papules progressing to grayish ring-formed patches with perifollicular papules. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are the three named variations of tinea pedis?","choices":["Superficial, deep and disseminated forms","Interdigital, hyperkeratotic, vesiculobullous","Annular, follicular and nodular forms","Acute, subacute and chronic forms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all three are caused by Trichophyton species, and the interdigital form is the most common. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What does the deck say about eradicating human papillomavirus with wart therapy?","choices":["Salicylic acid eradicates it reliably when used for six weeks","Surgical excision eradicates it reliably in immunocompetent patients","No therapy eradicates it with certainty, and recurrence can occur","Cryotherapy eradicates it reliably, so recurrence does not occur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — set expectations accordingly. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"Why does tinea barbae require oral therapy, and which agents are named?","choices":["Topical antifungals do not penetrate the hair follicle; griseofulvin or terbinafine","Topical antifungals are inactivated by beard sebum; fluconazole or itraconazole","The infection is systemic from the outset; intravenous amphotericin","Oral therapy is preferred only for cosmetic reasons; any azole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — with shaving or hair removal and warm compresses to lift crusts and debris. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the natural history of molluscum contagiosum in an immunocompetent patient?","choices":["None clear spontaneously, because the virus establishes lifelong latency in the skin","Most clear spontaneously, although resolution may take months to several years","Most clear spontaneously, always within two to three weeks of first appearing","Few clear spontaneously, so nearly every patient needs destructive treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — spontaneous but often slow. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 130"},{"q":"Which three genera account for the majority of dermatophyte infections?","choices":["Microsporum, Trichophyton, Epidermophyton","Candida, Malassezia, Trichophyton","Microsporum, Malassezia, Epidermophyton","Trichophyton, Aspergillus, Epidermophyton"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and infections are then classified by body location. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why does tinea capitis require oral rather than topical therapy?","choices":["The infection extends into the dermis, beyond topical reach","Children cannot tolerate the irritation of topical antifungals","Topical agents alone do not penetrate the infected hair shaft","Topical agents are inactivated by scalp sebum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — antifungal shampoo reduces spore shedding but does not replace oral therapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"When does herpes zoster scar?","choices":["When deeper epidermal or dermal layers are compromised by excoriation or secondary infection","Always, because the virus itself destroys the basement membrane wherever the lesions appear on the skin","Only in patients who go on to develop postherpetic neuralgia, whatever the depth of the lesions","Only when antiviral therapy is started later than 72 hours after the rash appears"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it typically heals without visible sequelae otherwise. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which organism causes most onychomycosis, and what does distal lateral disease produce?","choices":["Trichophyton rubrum; debris, onycholysis, thickening and crumbling","Candida albicans; a smooth white surface change without thickening","Malassezia species; yellow-gold fluorescence of the nail plate","Epidermophyton floccosum; pitting and oil-drop discoloration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — with discoloration too. Yeasts and moulds also occur, but dermatophytes cause most cases. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"When should resistant dermatophytosis be suspected?","choices":["Only in immunocompromised patients","Widespread, intensely inflammatory, linked, or terbinafine-refractory","When the lesion clears centrally within two weeks of starting treatment","Whenever a potassium hydroxide preparation is negative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — epidemiologically linked cases and failure of an adequate terbinafine course both count. Obtain species identification and susceptibility testing when available. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
